  "answer": "This has been reported before. Keyboard Manager only blocks the exact shortcut you remap. If you hold Alt+Tab and press a third key such as W, the combination no longer matches the remap, so Windows handles it and the Alt+Tab switcher opens. The maintainers say this is by design. The related duplicate tickets were closed in favor of the open feature request #21850, which asks for an option to block or allow fallthrough for these combinations. As a workaround, add a separate Disabled remap for each specific combination you want blocked, for example Alt+W+Tab. Keyboard Manager supports only a limited number of keys per shortcut, so this can't cover every combination. You can also add your thumbs-up to #21850."
 },
